"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was 3.8, and the surgical pathology showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with positive surgical margins, but no extraprostatic extension or seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty as nodal sampling was not performed. The biopsy finding of Acinar adenocarcinoma was documented, and the surgical findings indicated positive margins.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the lack of extraprostatic extension. The primary uncertainty stems from the missing postoperative PSA and subsequent trajectory, which is critical for assessing recurrence risk. Missing nodal sampling also contributes to this uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes would significantly revise the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 39.17 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 25.46–52.87 months; its coverage has not been validated."
